WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:07.200
What is a cloud shell, and how can a cloud&nbsp;
shell enable rapid cloud resource management and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:15.680
development from anywhere? I'm Nathan Heckman from&nbsp;
IBM Cloud and I'm going to answer that today. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:19.120
let's say you just finished&nbsp;
developing an exciting new app&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:26.080
and you decide to go on a&nbsp;
well-earned vacation. So, this is you

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:36.240
and you're sitting on a beach enjoying yourself&nbsp;
when all of a sudden you get a call from your boss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:42.080
and they're not happy because that&nbsp;
application just went down in production.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:49.680
So what do you do? You don't have your work&nbsp;
computer with you on the beach. Enter cloud shell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:57.200
You log into one of the hotel computers, you&nbsp;
open up a web browser, and with cloud shell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.800
you're able to fix the app in no time at all. So&nbsp;
that you can go back to enjoying your vacation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:09.760
and your boss is happy. So let's compare&nbsp;
some of the benefits of cloud shell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:18.000
with working with your local&nbsp;
machine. In a shell, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:26.880
with your local machine there's some limitations,&nbsp;
right. So first of all, inherently you're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:33.040
to use more resources on your CPU and memory,&nbsp;
it's just more resource intensive because it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:43.680
actually on your machine. You also need to worry&nbsp;
about installing software and updating software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:50.960
and tools. This can be annoying, right. Everyone's&nbsp;
been there where you're constantly prompted for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.600
updates and you need to keep things to the&nbsp;
latest version in order to keep them working.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:02.160
And finally if you're working with a cloud&nbsp;
provider you're always getting logged out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:10.800
so you have to manage your authentication,&nbsp;
your tokens, etc. Well with cloud shell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.920
it's a consistent environment.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:26.720
What does this mean? So first of all, all of&nbsp;
your cloud authentication is taken care of.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.800
So you're securely authenticated to your&nbsp;
cloud provider without doing anything.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:40.560
Also, you don't need to worry about&nbsp;
installing tools, everything is already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:49.840
installed and ready to go. And finally, maybe&nbsp;
most helpful, is there's no need to update.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.520
So everything's on the latest version and&nbsp;
you can get started and get right to work.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:04.720
Another benefit of cloud shell is&nbsp;
that you can manage your cloud.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:13.280
So, what can you manage? Well&nbsp;
your resources and infrastructure.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:23.200
You can manage your applications and you can&nbsp;
view logs. So, if you're sitting on a beach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:32.160
open up a cloud shell you can check the status&nbsp;
of your applications and get it back to working.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:38.400
A final benefit of cloud shell is that&nbsp;
it's a cloud based dev environment.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:47.200
So what does this mean for you? Cloud&nbsp;
shells include all the tools that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:52.560
need to be productive as a developer. So,&nbsp;
tools like git for source control management.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:58.000
Cloud shells typically include&nbsp;
several languages as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.840
So say you are a node developer, you&nbsp;
can use node right within a cloud shell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:15.120
And finally, cloud shells typically include an&nbsp;
editor. So everyone uses or knows of vim or nano.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:22.960
So you can actually edit files and scripts and&nbsp;
code right within your cloud shell environment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:30.400
Great. So now let's step back a bit&nbsp;
and talk about what is a cloud shell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.440
what are some of the building&nbsp;
blocks of a cloud shell.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:37.520
So, first of all,

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:43.920
at the base of what runs&nbsp;
cloud shell is a run time,

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:51.760
or VM. This is what powers&nbsp;
cloud shell at the lowest level.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:05:05.840
Next is storage. So, cloud shells may have&nbsp;
either persisted or temporary storage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:10.800
Persisted meaning as you go from session&nbsp;
to session, maybe you log out log back in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:16.240
you'll get your same environment and same storage.&nbsp;
Temporary, obviously. It's temporary, so the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:23.840
storage will be removed after some time.&nbsp;
And finally you have the cloud shell image.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:36.880
And this is what you interact with, right. So,&nbsp;
this could be some bash based shell. It includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:46.000
your tools and languages. So, this is what you&nbsp;
actually interact with on a cloud shell. Now of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:55.920
course all of this you access from a web browser.&nbsp;
So, it's super convenient and always available.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:01.120
Something to note too about cloud&nbsp;
shells is that they can either be

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.080
managed by you the user.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:13.920
And that means that you actually will provide&nbsp;
the runtime, storage, and image. So you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:23.920
configure that exactly how you want it, or a&nbsp;
cloud shell can be managed by the cloud vendor.

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:32.400
So what that means is they set up everything&nbsp;
for you, right. There's some benefits of both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:37.440
with the cloud vendor managed cloud&nbsp;
shell, everything's already ready to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:43.520
and cloud shell runtime, storage, image, they&nbsp;
already curate the tools and languages that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:48.560
are included and can be really convenient.&nbsp;
User managed can also have some benefits too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:53.680
Maybe there's some security&nbsp;
requirements from your enterprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:58.720
about where storage needs to be located, so&nbsp;
you can actually configure that yourself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:04.080
You can also configure exactly which tools and&nbsp;
languages you want to start up with cloud shell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:15.840
Great. So there's a few other features that cloud&nbsp;
shells could include. First one is web preview.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:23.040
What this is it essentially allows&nbsp;
you to preview a port on cloud shell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:28.720
similarly to how you do on your local&nbsp;
machine. So maybe port 3000 or port&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:33.760
8080 when you're doing local development you&nbsp;
get that same experience but on the cloud.

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:44.560
The next feature of a cloud shell is, that it&nbsp;
may or may not have, is a GUI based editor.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:50.880
Now pretty self-explanatory,&nbsp;
this is rather than a text editor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:56.880
like a Vim or Nano, it's actually a GUI where&nbsp;
you can go in and edit files or scripts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:01.840
it may even have a debugger. It can be very&nbsp;
useful when you're developing with cloud shell.

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:07.840
And finally, cloud shells could have

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:19.520
file upload or download. So, also pretty&nbsp;
self-explanatory, it allows you to actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:24.000
upload files from your local computer to&nbsp;
the cloud shell environment, so that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:29.040
you go to somewhere else some other computer or&nbsp;
workstation you can see those same files there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:34.720
It is also for file download, you can&nbsp;
take files or scripts from cloud shell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:39.840
environment down to your local machine and&nbsp;
continue development there if you so choose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:47.840
So that's what cloud shell is and those are some&nbsp;
of the benefits. As you can see cloud shell is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:54.720
powerful consistent environment, it's your own&nbsp;
personal workspace on the cloud. Thank you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

